# Reportly — Cursor rules

## Project
Repo: `reportly`. Product: **Reportly**, a rule-first complaint assistant on CFPB (English) data.

- UI chrome may be Turkish.
- Customer messages, rules, intents, bot replies, and ticket text are English.
- Do not machine-translate the CFPB dataset to “make it Turkish.”

## Stack (do not change without asking)
Python 3.10+, Streamlit, scikit-learn (TF-IDF + logistic regression), JSONL tickets.
No TypeScript, no extra web framework, no database unless explicitly requested.

## Architecture
Keep these layers separate; do not dump logic into `app.py`:

- `nlu_engine.py` — category model
- `sentiment.py` / `intents.py` / `rules.py` — signals and procedures
- `orchestrator.py` — turn decision (the brain)
- `tickets.py` / `summaries.py` — persistence and manager brief
- `auth.py` — local JSONL accounts (customer vs admin)
- `app.py` — UI only

The LLM (`llm_analyze.py`, Ollama) is optional and must not decide escalate vs resolve. Default summaries are templates.

## Product rules
- Try a self-serve rule first.
- Open a ticket when no rule matches, the rule rarely resolves, the issue is high-risk (fraud / identity theft / legal / foreclosure), the customer says it did not help, or they ask to file a complaint.
- Angry tone offers a complaint; it does not silently open a ticket unless high-risk.
- A ticket carries a manager brief plus conversation notes so a supervisor can read the story without a live chat handoff.

## Coding
- Prefer small, readable Python. Type hints on new public functions.
- Do not add packages without asking.
- Do not expand to RFM, forecasting, CRM, or semantic clustering unless asked.
- Before large refactors, check `PRD.md` and `ARCHITECTURE.md` (current vs later).
